## Estate shell history: never lose bash history again
# One private repo (slade208/shell-history) holds every enrolled host's bash
# history: per-host capture with timestamps, a generated HISTORY.md with
# ==host== sections, and a hot-set window so Ctrl-R on any host finds
# commands run on any other. Every command hits disk immediately - crashed
# sessions lose nothing. Desktop is the regen owner.

## Am I enrolled?
echo $HISTFILE
# enrolled     -> ~/.shell-history/hosts/<hostname>.log
# not enrolled -> ~/.bash_history (the default)
# NOTE: $HISTFILE only updates in NEW shells - for the machine's true state:
grep -c 'shell-history' ~/.bashrc   # 0 = not enrolled, >0 = enrolled

## Enrol this host (any Linux box; idempotent)
# Requirements on the host: git, curl, ssh-keygen (the script CHECKS and OFFERS
# to install anything missing - dnf/yum/apt/zypper/apk/pacman). NO GitHub tooling -
# the gh CLI is optional and only makes enrolment one-step.
bash -c "$(curl -fsSL operations.dev/hist-join.sh)"
# Three ways to register the key, most-scoped first:
#   A) LEAST PRIVILEGE - enrolment token (recommended). Mint a fine-grained
#      PAT: github Settings -> Developer settings -> Fine-grained tokens ->
#      Generate new; Only select repositories -> shell-history; Repository
#      permissions -> Administration: Read and write; expiry 7-30 days.
#      Then:  HIST_ENROL_TOKEN=<token> bash -c "$(curl -fsSL operations.dev/hist-join.sh)"
#      (or paste it at the script's hidden prompt). Plain curl adds the key -
#      no gh needed, token used once in memory, nothing stored on the host.
#      Blast radius if leaked: admin of the ONE history repo, until expiry.
#   B) browser (any device): github.com/slade208/shell-history/settings/keys/new
#      title: <hostname> - tick 'Allow write access', then re-run.
#   C) gh CLI: gh auth login (device flow - approve from your phone), re-run.
#      CAUTION: gh login = a FULL-ACCOUNT token on the host; the script now
#      offers 'gh auth logout' right after the key is added - say yes. The
#      deploy key is all enrolment needs long-term.

## Daily use
# ~/.shell-history/tooling/histgrep <pattern> [host]   search the whole estate
# Ctrl-R                                               includes other hosts' recent commands
# cat ~/.shell-history/HISTORY.md                      the whole estate, sectioned

## Restore
# Damaged local file:  git -C ~/.shell-history restore hosts/$(hostname -s).log
# Reimaged host:       re-run the enrol command - first shell has full history
# Point in time:       git -C ~/.shell-history log -p hosts/<host>.log

## Leave (voluntary, run ON the host)
# ~/.shell-history/tooling/leave.sh          remove hooks, keep clone
# ~/.shell-history/tooling/leave.sh --purge  also delete clone + key

## Evict a host (lost laptop, compromised or decommissioned box - run ANYWHERE)
# Revoking its deploy key instantly cuts that host's read+push; all other
# hosts are untouched. From any gh-authed seat:
#   gh repo deploy-key list -R slade208/shell-history            find the ID
#   gh repo deploy-key delete <ID> -R slade208/shell-history     revoke
# Or browser: github.com/slade208/shell-history/settings/keys
# The host's history STAYS in the repo (that's the point). To erase the
# record too: git rm hosts/<host>.log in any clone, commit, push.
